# Relevance notes keyed by the dump index [n]. Theme codes:
# A structured/correlated arms (mean channel); B temporally dependent rewards (fluctuation channel);
# C confidence sequences and inference after adaptive sampling (bridge); D online experiments and batched exposure;
# E dependence across units (network interference).
THEMES = {
    "A": "Structured and correlated arms: graphs, clusters, similarity (mean channel; A1, A4, N1)",
    "B": "Temporally dependent and non-stationary rewards (fluctuation channel; S5, P1, B3)",
    "C": "Confidence sequences, anytime-valid inference, and inference after adaptive sampling (bridge; B1, B2)",
    "D": "Online experiments, A/B testing, and limited adaptivity (bursty exposure; B2, B3)",
    "E": "Dependence across units: network interference (spatial correlation of rewards)",
}
NO_INTRO = {56, 59, 60, 81, 90, 93}  # PDFs not reachable; abstract only

N = {}
def n(i, theme, core, text):
    N[i] = (theme, core, text)

# ---------- A: structured and correlated arms ----------
n(23, "A", True, "Hierarchical similarity over arms (nested partitions by traits) constrains within-group reward ranges and yields regret that improves with the hierarchy. The closest recent theory to our certified diameters: their partition-level range bounds are our ε_c, but assumed known and valid rather than certified from data.")
n(31, "A", True, "Multi-user contextual bandits with a user graph: a joint penalty (graph smoothness in RKHS distance plus ridge) is shown to be a single multi-user RKHS whose kernel fuses the Laplacian with the arm kernel; regret scales with an effective dimension. The kernelized form of Gang-of-Bandits/spectral smoothing, with no validity check of the graph; our rewired-null and certificate tests apply directly.")
n(84, "A", False, "Link prediction as a contextual bandit fused with PageRank over the graph for exploitation and exploration. Uses graph connectivity as a prior for reward aggregation, uncertified; relevant as a KDD-audience precedent for graphs in bandits.")
n(72, "A", False, "Contexts (users) lumpable into r groups with identical mean rewards; near-optimal sample complexity and regret in r(S+K). The exact-equality version of our component model (ε_c = 0) with unknown groups.")
n(71, "A", False, "Users in latent clusters with identical reward vectors, each item recommendable at most B times per user; matrix-completion-based collaboration. Latent-cluster pooling with equal means, no diameter slack and no validity certificate.")
n(76, "A", True, "Clustering of bandits where users in a cluster share the linear part of the reward but deviate by up to ε_*; robust graph- and set-based clustering with regret O(ε_* T √(md log T) + d√(mT) log T). Their per-user deviation is a within-cluster diameter that enters regret linearly, which is the misalignment cost N1 and A1 describe; they tolerate it rather than reject components.")
n(28, "A", True, "Shows that UCB-based online clustering of bandits lacks the exploration needed to identify clusters and needs strong context-diversity assumptions; adds an exploration phase (UniCLUB, UniSCLUB). Relevant to our CLUB-style control: the clustering baseline's weakness is in cluster identification, which certified pooling sidesteps by taking the partition from a mined graph and certifying it.")
n(42, "A", False, "Best-arm identification for N agents in M unknown clusters sharing bandit instances; cluster-then-identify and identify-then-cluster with minimax sample complexity for constant M. Pure-exploration analogue of Setting B's user-side pooling, without a diameter slack.")
n(58, "A", True, "Clustering of neural bandits (M-CNB): a meta-learner represents dynamic user clusters with arbitrary reward functions, UCB exploration, instance-dependent guarantee. KDD 2024; the KDD-side state of the art for pooling across users. It pools whatever the clustering finds and has no validity guarantee for the pooled estimate.")
n(66, "A", True, "KDD 2025 follow-up: clustering of neural bandits loses plasticity in non-stationary environments; selective reinitialization with change detection restores sublinear regret in piecewise-stationary settings, tested on six recommendation datasets. The KDD precedent that combines pooling across users with non-stationarity, treated as abrupt change rather than persistence.")
n(59, "A", False, "KDD 2024, deployed at scale: Neural Linear Bandit with multi-task embeddings and approximate uncertainty, live experiments on a commercial recommender. Evidence that KDD values uncertainty-driven exploration in production recommenders; no structure between arms beyond shared embeddings. (Abstract only.)")
n(63, "A", False, "KDD 2025: budget allocation across ad lines as a multi-task combinatorial bandit with a Bayesian hierarchical model that shares information through campaign metadata; Thompson sampling; Amazon campaign data. Hierarchical shrinkage is the graph-free pooling that our experiments found to carry most of the gain.")
n(81, "A", False, "Improved Bayes regret for hierarchical Thompson sampling and a hierarchical BayesUCB in multi-task linear and semi-bandits. Theory for the shrinkage baseline (tasks drawn from a shared prior). (Abstract only.)")
n(85, "A", True, "Mobile-health contextual bandit with user- and time-specific random effects, network-cohesion penalties across users, and debiased baseline estimation; regret depends only on the differential-reward dimension. Combines a user graph penalty with time effects, which is the closest applied analogue of our two-channel model; the graph is used as a prior, not certified.")
n(73, "A", False, "Auxiliary feedback correlated with the reward (a control variate with known mean) tightens confidence bounds and reduces regret by a factor depending on the correlation coefficient. Correlation across observations of the same arm, not across arms; the mechanism (known correlation reduces variance) parallels B3.2's contrast gain.")
n(32, "A", True, "Graph-triggered rising bandits: a graph over arms says which arms' reward evolution a pull triggers; restless and rested are the two extreme graphs; optimal policy NP-hard in general, closed form for cliques. A graph that shapes dynamics rather than means, which is our second graph role, under rested (action-driven) rather than exogenous evolution.")
n(68, "A", False, "KDD 2026: adversarial bandits over a metric configuration space partitioned into clusters with Lipschitz rewards within clusters; a bandit over bandits with an O(√(kT)) safety net and faster learning under Lipschitz structure. Cluster-level pooling with a worst-case fallback, the adversarial counterpart of certified pooling's 'never worse than per-arm' structure.")

# ---------- B: temporally dependent rewards ----------
n(1, "B", True, "Rewards follow an AR(k) process whose coefficients depend on the chosen action; the optimal policy is stationary and closed-loop; AR-UCB has regret Õ((k+1)^{3/2}√(nT)/(1−Γ)^2). Action-dependent AR dynamics against our exogenous AR(p) fields; the (1−Γ)^{-2} persistence cost matches the (1−φ)^{-2} factor in B1's corollary.")
n(75, "B", True, "Non-stationary MAB with an AR reward structure; alternation plus restarting; near-matching regret against a dynamic benchmark; tourism demand case study. The paper already cited as O11; it has no mean-channel structure and no confidence guarantee for fixed means.")
n(15, "B", True, "Rewards bilinear in the action and a latent state with unknown stable linear dynamics driven by the actions; explore-then-commit with Õ(pT^{2/3}) regret; the exploration phase must learn from temporally correlated rewards. Endogenous latent dynamics; our dynamics are exogenous, but their system-identification error bounds under correlated rewards are the kind of estimated-dynamics result our certificate needs.")
n(97, "B", True, "Same latent-linear-dynamics model; block-level optimism with a finite-memory cyclic approximation gives Õ(√T). Shows that under stable dynamics the infinite-memory reward process can be truncated, which is the same geometric-decay argument behind our long-run substitution (B3.1).")
n(45, "B", False, "Linear contextual bandits whose contexts and rewards are governed by a finite hidden Markov chain; reduction to linear bandits via beliefs, with the HMM estimation error entering the regret; periodic parameter updates handle the dependence. A Markov-state version of persistence with forgetting conditions; relevant to how estimated dynamics enter guarantees.")
n(67, "B", True, "KDD 2026: rewards depend on an unobserved Markov state evolving independently of actions; LinUCB fed with lagged action-reward pairs and probe fingerprints from several arms; synthetic stress tests. The KDD-side version of exogenous latent dynamics; the probe fingerprint is a slate-style joint observation, as in our B3.2.")
n(50, "B", False, "Generalized linear bandits with memory: rewards depend on past actions through a finite memory matrix; block-wise algorithm with shrunken confidence bounds reaches √T rates. Endogenous memory over structured action spaces; the block-wise confidence construction (commit to a block, update after) is a bursty-exposure design.")
n(96, "B", True, "Best-arm identification when all arms' means share an arbitrary, possibly adversarial common shift each round while gaps are stable; GLRT stopping rules such as Track-and-Stop fail; importance weights restore δ-correctness. The common shift is exactly the common factor our Wikipedia and KuaiRec panels show carries most of the graph-covariance gain (S6); their failure of GLRT stopping under shifts is a decision-level analogue of B2.")
n(0, "B", True, "A/B testing and best-arm identification in linear bandits when arm values change arbitrarily over time; randomized designs with IPS estimators identify the counterfactual best treatment. Motivation overlaps ours (hour-to-hour non-stationarity in experiments); their fix is randomization against adversarial variation, ours is modelling persistence.")
n(24, "B", False, "Complexity of fixed-budget BAI in non-stationary linear bandits is governed by adjacent arm pairs, an arm-set-dependent geometry. Geometry beyond pairwise gaps determines difficulty, in the spirit of our path-versus-clique separation (A2), here for non-stationary identification.")
n(10, "B", False, "Non-stationary linear bandits with variance-dependent regret: using the reward variance as well as the variation budget B_K tightens dynamic regret. Variation-budget non-stationarity; no temporal correlation structure.")
n(4, "B", False, "Near-optimal non-stationary kernelized bandits (time-varying Bayesian optimization): first lower bounds for SE and Matérn kernels; restarting phased elimination with random permutation. With [7] and [9], the current theory of time-varying GP bandits, all under variation budgets rather than autocorrelated dynamics.")
n(7, "B", False, "First algorithm-independent lower bounds for time-varying kernelized bandits under total-variation budgets in sup and RKHS norms. See [4].")
n(9, "B", False, "Time-varying GP bandits when the prior (kernel) is unknown: reject priors inconsistent with the data, with a regret bound; real-world time-varying problems. Model selection among candidate dynamics models, which our estimated-dynamics certificates also need (lag-order selection is our open item 4).")
n(48, "B", False, "Detection Augmented Learning: any order-optimal stationary bandit algorithm plus a change detector, prior-free, for piecewise-stationary bandits of all kinds; extensive experiments including real data. Representative of the change-detection family we compare against (abrupt change rather than persistence).")
n(19, "B", False, "Piecewise-stationary restless bandits: arms are Markov chains whose mean rewards change across unknown segments; modular change detection with diminishing exploration; regret Õ(√(LMKT)) with mixing time L. Restless Markov arms with regime changes; the mixing time plays the role our long-run variance plays.")
n(80, "B", False, "Fixed-confidence BAI in piecewise-stationary linear bandits where contexts are resampled at change points; change detection and context alignment give near-optimal sample complexity. Irrevocable identification under non-stationarity, the decision type our B2 consequence concerns.")
n(78, "B", False, "Nonparametric (Lipschitz) contextual bandits with changing rewards; minimax dynamic regret in L and V, and adaptivity to 'experienced significant shifts' that count only changes relevant to observed contexts. Defines non-stationarity by its effect on decisions, which is how we present B2's consequence (only shifts that cross a gap matter).")
n(65, "B", True, "KDD 2025: Gaussian-interpolation bandit over a continuous arm space with a non-stationary extension, 100–10000× faster than sliding GP policies, on datasets with non-stationarity and very many arms. The KDD-side baseline for 'many arms plus drift'; similarity between arms is Lipschitz in a metric, not a mined graph, and time dependence is a window, not a model.")
n(64, "B", True, "KDD 2025: future off-policy evaluation and learning under non-stationarity, exploiting seasonal, weekly and holiday structure shared between past and future data through a new importance weighting. The same long-run time structure (weekly lags) that our inflation diagnostic needs, used for offline evaluation rather than online confidence.")
n(61, "B", False, "KDD 2025: Bayesian optimization over A/B tests that combines fast biased experiments and offline proxies with slow long-running ones, because short-term measurements mislead under non-stationary treatment effects. Practitioner evidence that treatment effects persist and drift over days, the regime of experiment proposal 1.")

# ---------- C: confidence and inference after adaptive sampling ----------
n(70, "C", True, "Tail bounds for adaptive martingale mixtures give confidence sequences for linear bandits that are tighter than OFUL's, computable by convex programming, with competitive worst-case regret. Tighter mixtures over the same self-normalized martingale we use in S2; a candidate for shrinking B1's log-determinant width (our top open problem).")
n(8, "C", True, "Martingale tail inequalities give confidence bounds for sequential kernel regression, computed through the dual of a conic program; always tighter than existing bounds; better KernelUCB. Companion to [70]; the dual computation is the practical route to per-contrast widths.")
n(74, "C", True, "Likelihood-ratio confidence sequences, any-time valid for well-specified likelihoods, with estimator sequences chosen by online learning and reweighting; non-asymptotic size for GLMs. Our innovation regression has an exact Gaussian likelihood, so these sets apply directly and may be narrower than the mixture bound.")
n(79, "C", True, "A unified likelihood-ratio confidence sequence for self-concordant GLMs, convex and numerically tight, with poly(S)-free radius for Bernoulli; OFUGLB. Relevant to the KL-UCB-style certificates in Setting A v2 and to a Bernoulli version of B1.")
n(36, "C", False, "Gambling-based confidence sequences for bounded random vectors via universal portfolios; tighter than existing vector CSs. Betting CSs for several arms at once, an alternative to our union bound over N+M scalar processes in B1′.")
n(95, "C", True, "NeurIPS 2026: tractable multivariate confidence sequences for bounded vector means (weighted Bonferroni, max-wealth, portfolio region with outer approximations) for multi-metric A/B testing and model comparison. The tightness/tractability trade-off of multivariate CSs is the same one between B1 (vector, log-det) and B1′ (scalar union).")
n(22, "C", True, "Time-uniform self-normalized concentration for vector-valued processes under a sub-ψ condition; rate √(log log γ_max(V_t) + d log κ(V_t)), tighter than the log-det method of mixtures when V_t is well conditioned. Directly addresses why B1's width is √(N log T): a condition-number bound could replace the log-determinant.")
n(25, "C", False, "COLT 2026: scale-invariant self-normalized martingale inequalities and their equivalence with doubly uniform regret in linear regression. Foundations for S2-type bounds; relevant to removing the regularization term's scale dependence.")
n(21, "C", False, "Information-theoretic proof that time-uniform estimation must pay a √(log log n) factor over fixed-n confidence. Sets the floor for any anytime-valid certificate: B1 cannot do better than LIL rates, but is currently far above them.")
n(77, "C", False, "Time-uniform, value-uniform CDF bounds for the running averaged conditional distribution of a dependent sequence; always valid, sometimes trivial; counterfactual version for A/B tests and bandits. Anytime-valid inference under arbitrary dependence at the cost of width, the same 'valid but vacuous' trade-off our report names.")
n(46, "C", True, "Asymptotically optimal α-correct power-one sequential tests for data from an ergodic finite-state Markov chain, with instance-dependent lower bounds on stopping time in terms of stationary-weighted KL. Sequential testing under Markovian dependence is the discrete-state counterpart of B1/B2; their stopping-time constants are what B3.1's long-run variance gives for Gaussian AR.")
n(91, "C", False, "Betting-based confidence intervals for bounded means that recalculate the target at each step; optimal width up to 1+o(1) in the fixed-horizon regime. Fixed-n (not time-uniform) betting; relevant to narrowing certificates when a horizon is known.")
n(51, "C", False, "Horizon-aware anytime-valid tests: Kelly betting is optimal in an interior region and more or less aggressive betting elsewhere; a learned DQN betting policy. The practical point that known deadlines allow narrower sequences than fully time-uniform ones applies to our fixed-horizon KuaiRec runs.")
n(54, "C", False, "A CLT for the stopping time of the KL_inf-based sequential mean test, giving second-order (variability) guarantees beyond the first-order optimal expected stopping time. For reporting stopping-time distributions of certified elimination, which our report asks for.")
n(34, "C", True, "PEAK: non-asymptotic sequential tests for composite hypotheses on means of multiple adaptively sampled streams by betting, avoiding union bounds through averaged capital; up to 85% fewer samples before stopping in pure-exploration bandits; HeartSteps data. A direct alternative to our union-bounded B1′ for component rejection, under iid streams.")
n(86, "C", True, "Bernstein–von Mises for adaptively collected data: Bayesian uncertainty asymptotically matches Wald-type frequentist UQ under stability, and is not frequentist-valid when stability fails. Explains why the Bayesian (Thompson/Gaussian) baselines in our experiments give calibrated-looking intervals in some regimes and not others.")
n(92, "C", False, "Simulation-based inference for adaptive experiments (simulation with optimism) gives asymptotic type-I control and 50% narrower intervals than martingale concentration. Asymptotic, design-specific inference as the alternative to anytime-valid certificates; the width gap it reports is the gap our report measures (10–25×).")
n(83, "C", False, "Non-asymptotic MSE bounds for AIPW estimators from adaptively collected data, with the sequentially weighted estimation error minimized by online learning. Inference from adaptively collected data for off-policy value; complements our confidence-for-fixed-means question.")
n(44, "C", False, "Optimistic adaptive sampling (OPTrack) for ATE estimation with the AIPW estimator; finite-sample analysis; oversample arms that will later be under-sampled. Adaptive experimental design for inference rather than regret; the optimism principle transfers to information-directed certification.")
n(52, "C", False, "Prediction-powered adaptive inference: combines adaptively collected labels, unlabeled data and pretrained predictions in one estimating equation with asymptotic normality. Auxiliary predictions as extra information under adaptive sampling; adjacent to our use of mined graphs as prior information.")
n(11, "C", False, "Kernel treatment effects (distributional) under adaptive data collection, with sample-split sequential normalization. Shows the breakage of iid asymptotics under adaptive logging that motivates all of theme C.")

# ---------- D: online experiments, A/B tests, limited adaptivity ----------
n(12, "D", True, "Period-level treatment-arm means modelled as autoregressive state-space processes; a Kalman-smoother estimator of the time-averaged effect, response-adaptive allocation using predicted Kalman variances, asymptotic normality, dominance over the naive estimator, and anytime-valid confidence sequences. The nearest neighbour to our experiment proposal 1 and to B1 on daily aggregates; it treats one two-arm test with a known AR(1) and does not study the failure of iid sequences (B2) or pooling across tests.")
n(53, "D", True, "Robust sequential experimental design for A/B tests under model misspecification, covering contextual-bandit and dynamic settings with carryover effects; bounds worst-case MSE; real data from a technology company. Carryover between periods is the mechanism behind our bursty-exposure inflation; they design allocations, we certify confidence.")
n(49, "D", True, "Design-based anytime-valid inference with delayed outcomes and staggered entry: the IPW treatment-effect error is not a martingale under any filtration, but per-arm errors are, so a union of per-arm confidence sequences is used. The same per-arm-then-union construction as our B1′, arrived at for a different reason; their estimand is design-based and robust to nonstationarity.")
n(5, "D", False, "Anytime-valid confidence processes for cumulative rates of two counting processes and an e-process for equality of rates, for monitoring adverse events in experiments. Continuous-time sequential monitoring in A/B tests; iid increments.")
n(94, "D", True, "YEAST: a continuous sequential test for A/B experiments derived by inverting a maximal-inequality bound; better type-I control and power than existing continuous monitoring methods; semi-synthetic validation on a public dataset. A representative iid sequential test that experiment proposal 1 would run on autocorrelated daily metrics.")
n(56, "D", True, "KDD 2024: with 10–20% true success rates, α = 0.05 implies many false positive A/B wins; estimates true success rates from observed win rates at Expedia and Optimizely; proposes sequential group testing that extends experiments selectively. KDD's own evidence that false positives in experimentation matter, which B2 adds a mechanism to. (Abstract only.)")
n(60, "D", False, "KDD 2025: online experiments with diminishing marginal effects of traffic allocation; an algorithm that attains optimal cumulative reward and a valid CLT for inference at the unknown optimal allocation. Regret-and-inference trade-off in platform experiments; iid across users. (Abstract only.)")
n(33, "D", False, "For fixed-budget A/B testing (two Bernoulli arms) no algorithm beats uniform sampling on all instances; for more arms, uniform sampling sometimes beats Successive Rejects. A caution for adaptive allocation in two-arm tests; our proposal 1 uses fixed allocation with sequential stopping.")
n(13, "D", False, "Pareto-optimal trade-off between regret and estimation error of reward gaps in combinatorial bandits under full-bandit and semi-bandit feedback. The regret-versus-inference frontier that certified pooling sits on (correct decisions at a regret cost).")
n(26, "D", False, "Optimal batch-regret trade-off for batch linear contextual bandits for all parameter ranges, with a new matrix concentration inequality under dynamic upper bounds. Batched (bursty) exposure as a constraint; iid noise within batches.")
n(27, "D", False, "Batched best-arm identification with instance-sensitive batch complexity below log(1/Δ_2), on synthetic and MovieLens data. Batched identification under iid rewards; B2 says consecutive batches of persistent rewards break the iid confidence these methods use.")
n(47, "D", False, "Contextual slate bandits with GLM rewards under batched and rarely-switching limited adaptivity; O(log log T) batches; κ-free regret. Slates with limited adaptivity, our bursty-exposure regime, again with independent rewards.")

# ---------- E: network interference ----------
n(82, "E", True, "MAB where each unit's reward depends on the treatments of s neighbours in a network (sparse interference); a Fourier sparse-linear representation gives regret Õ((A^s T)^{2/3}) with unknown neighbourhoods. Dependence across units through treatments; our dependence is in the rewards' shocks. Both are 'spatial' correlation a graph encodes, in different channels.")
n(40, "E", False, "Linear contextual bandits with interference across units in the same round; algorithms that quantify the interference effect with regret, finite-sample and asymptotic guarantees; MovieLens-based synthetic data. See [82].")
n(41, "E", True, "Multi-armed bandits with interference where interference decays with distance and rewards are adversarial; switchback policies are optimal in expectation but high-variance in N; clustered randomization fixes it. Spatially decaying interference and clustered designs mirror our graph-correlated shocks and component structure; their switchbacks are bursty exposure by design.")
n(17, "E", False, "Scalable Thompson sampling for policy learning on large networks with interference, with nodes and edges sampled each period and Bayesian regret bounds. Addresses the fixed-network, temporally independent assumptions of earlier interference bandits, the same two assumptions our model relaxes.")
n(88, "E", True, "Design-based (adversarial) bandits under network interference with a Pareto frontier between regret and inference accuracy and an anytime-valid asymptotic confidence sequence (EXP3-N-CS). Anytime-valid inference plus network dependence in one paper; their confidence sequence is asymptotic, ours exact under Gaussian dynamics.")
n(69, "E", False, "KDD 2026: learning peer-influence probabilities with linear contextual bandits; a regret–estimation-error trade-off with all achievable rate pairs; uncertainty-guided exploration. KDD-side evidence that estimation under network dependence is a recognized problem.")
